Here's a simple SystemVerilog module for an LDPC (Low-Density Parity-Check) encoder for an FPGA implementation:

```verilog
module ldpc_encoder(
    input wire clk,
    input wire reset,
    input wire data_in,
    output wire data_out
);

reg [7:0] state;
reg [7:0] parity;

always @(posedge clk or posedge reset)
begin
    if (reset)
    begin
        state <= 8'b00000000;
        parity <= 8'b00000000;
    end else
    begin
        state <= state + 1;
        parity <= parity ^ data_in;
    end
end

assign data_out = state[7];

endmodule
```

This module implements a simple LDPC encoder with a 8-bit input and output data. The state register is used to keep track of the current state of the encoder, while the parity register is used to store the parity of the input data. The `data_out` signal is set to the most significant bit of the state register.

Note that this is a very basic implementation of an LDPC encoder and may not be suitable for all FPGA applications. For more advanced LDPC encoders, you may want to consider using a more complex design or a pre-designed IP core.

