In ASP.NET Core Web API, you can retrieve the user name from the JWT token using the `User` property of the `HttpContext`. Here's an example:

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authorization;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;
using Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens;
using System.Security.Claims;

namespace YourProjectName.Controllers
{
    [Authorize]
    [Route("api/[controller]")]
    [ApiController]
    public class MyController : ControllerBase
    {
        [HttpGet]
        public ActionResult Get()
        {
            var user = User;
            var userName = user.Claims.FirstOrDefault(c => c.Type == ClaimTypes.Name)?.Value;

            // Use the user name here
            return Ok($"Hello, {userName}!");
        }
    }
}
```

Make sure to add the `Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens` package to your project if you haven't already. You can do this by running the following command in the terminal or command prompt:

```
dotnet add package Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens
```

Also, make sure that your JWT token contains the `Name` claim with the user name. This claim is typically included in the token when the user logs in and the token is issued.

