```matlab
% Define population size and traits
pop_size = 100;
traits = [0.5 0.7 0.9 0.3 0.1]; % Traits range from 0 to 1

% Create initial population
population = zeros(pop_size, 1);
for i = 1:pop_size
    population(i) = traits(randi([1 numel(traits)]));
end

% Define fitness function
fitness = @(x) x^2; % Fitness is proportional to the square of the trait value

% Simulate natural selection
for i = 1:100 % Run for 100 generations
    % Evaluate fitness of individuals
    fitness_values = fitness(population);

    % Select parents based on fitness
    parent_indices = sort(fitness_values, 'descend');
    parents = population(parent_indices);

    % Create offspring by combining parents' traits
    offspring = parents * 0.5;

    % Replace population with offspring
    population = offspring;
end

% Display final population
disp('Final population:');
disp(population);
```

This code simulates the evolution of a population over 100 generations, where the fitness of each individual is determined by their trait value. The code selects parents based on their fitness and creates offspring by combining their traits. Over time, the population evolves to become more fit, with individuals having higher trait values surviving and reproducing.

To call a PowerShell script from Terraform that allows read-host input, you can use the `local-exec` provisioner in Terraform. Here's an example of how to do this:

1. Create a PowerShell script (e.g., `read_input.ps1`) with the code you want to execute. This script should prompt the user for input using `Read-Host`.
2. In your Terraform configuration file (e.g., `main.tf`), define a `local-exec` block to call the PowerShell script. Here's an example:
```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-west-2"
}

resource "aws_instance" "example" {
  ami           = "ami-0c55b159cbfafe1f0"
  instance_type = "t2.micro"

  provisioner "local-exec" {
    command = "powershell -File read_input.ps1"
  }
}
```
This will run the `read_input.ps1` script on the local machine when the AWS instance is created. The output of the script will be displayed in the Terraform output.

Note that you may need to install the PowerShell module required by your script using `Install-Module` before running the script. You can do this by adding a `pre-install` block to the `local-exec` provisioner, like this:
```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-west-2"
}

resource "aws_instance" "example" {
  ami           = "ami-0c55b159cbfafe1f0"
  instance_type = "t2.micro"

  provisioner "local-exec" {
    command = "powershell -Module MyModule -File read_input.ps1"
    pre-install = "powershell -InstallModule MyModule"
  }
}
```
