Here's a decorator for Nest.js middleware that checks the `NODE_ENV` variable and calls `next()` if it's equal to 'true', but returns `false` if it's equal to 'false':

```typescript
import { Injectable, CanActivate, ExecutionContext } from '@nestjs/common';

@Injectable()
export class EnvMiddleware implements CanActivate {
  canActivate(context: ExecutionContext): boolean {
    const req = context.switchToHttp().getRequest();
    const nodeEnv = req.nodeEnv;

    if (nodeEnv === 'true') {
      return true;
    } else if (nodeEnv === 'false') {
      return false;
    } else {
      throw new Error('Invalid NODE_ENV value');
    }
  }
}
```

You can use this middleware in your Nest.js application by adding it to the `middleware` array in your `app.module.ts` file:

```typescript
import { MiddlewareFunction, NestModule } from '@nestjs/common';
import { EnvMiddleware } from './env.middleware';

@NestModule({
  imports: [],
  controllers: [],
  providers: [],
  middleware: [EnvMiddleware],
})
export class AppModule {}
```

This middleware will be executed before any other middleware in your application, and it will check the `NODE_ENV` variable to determine whether to allow the request to proceed or to return an error.

