To solve the issue of planets being too close together, you can implement a simple collision detection system and adjust the position of the newly generated planet if it collides with an existing one. Here's an updated version of your `generateRandomPlanets` function:

```javascript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
  const planets = [];
  let attempts = 0;

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++;

    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    };

    // Check if planet is too close to any existing planet
    for (let i = 0; i < planets.length; i++) {
      const otherPlanet = planets[i];
      const distance = planet.position.reduce((a, b) => a + b * b, 0);

      if (distance < minDistance * minDistance) {
        continue;
      }

      planets.push(planet);
      break;
    }
  }

  return planets;
};
```

This function will now check if the newly generated planet collides with any existing planet before adding it to the list. If it does, it will skip that planet and try again. This should help prevent planets from being too close together.

Here's a simple React utility that removes widows from text. It uses the `React.useState` hook to keep track of the input text and the resulting text without widows.

```javascript
import React, { useState } from 'react';

const RemoveWidows = ({ text }) => {
  const [resultText, setResultText] = useState('');

  const handleInputChange = (event) => {
    const inputText = event.target.value;
    const result = inputText.replace(/\B(\w)\b/g, '$1\u24D2'); // Replace word boundary with non-breaking space character
    setResultText(result);
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <textarea value={text} onChange={handleInputChange} />
      <p>{resultText}</p>
    </div>
  );
};

export default RemoveWidows;
```

To use this utility, simply pass the input text as a prop:

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import RemoveWidows from './RemoveWidows';

const App = () => {
  const text = 'This is an example text with some words separated by spaces.';

  return (
    <div>
      <RemoveWidows text={text} />
    </div>
  );
};

export default App;
```

This utility will replace all word boundaries in the input text with non-breaking space characters, effectively removing widows. The resulting text will be displayed below the input text.

